Start with the selected file's settings and conversion log. A problem that affects one source often comes from that file's streams, output format, or a specific processing option.

## Frame will not open

Current macOS builds are not notarized, and Windows builds are not certificate signed. Follow the platform-specific steps in [installation](/docs/installation) for a copy from the official release page.

For Linux, check that the package matches your processor architecture. If you use an AppImage, the file must be executable. The Flathub package uses its runtime for media tools.

## The main action does not say Start

The action tells you what is missing. **Add a source** means the queue is empty; **Select files** means no files are checked; **Choose output** means a destination is needed. **Nothing pending** means there is no selected pending conversion to run.

See [batch conversion](/docs/batch-conversion) for the queue workflow.

## A source file cannot be read

If Frame shows **Preview unavailable** and **Failed to read source metadata**, it could not inspect the source. The error details can include a failed FFprobe process.

Check that the original file opens in a media player and that its download or copy finished. A supported filename extension does not guarantee valid media inside the file. Try a known working source to distinguish a file problem from an installation problem.

<DocsScreenshot
  scene="source-error"
  alt="Frame showing Preview unavailable and Failed to read source metadata for unreadable-sample.mp4."
  caption="A failed source probe appears in the preview and the Metadata tab."
/>

## A source or setting is unavailable

Check the supported formats in the [video](/docs/video), [audio](/docs/audio), or [image](/docs/images) guide. Some image formats, including AVIF and HEIC, are accepted as inputs without being offered as outputs.

The chosen container limits codecs and track types. Try a compatible preset first, then adjust one setting at a time. A disabled preset may not apply to the selected source type.

## Audio or subtitles are missing

Review the checked source tracks in the Output inspector. Frame exports selected tracks only. Then confirm that the output container supports the track format.

Selectable subtitles also require support in the player. If they must always be visible, consider [burn-in](/docs/subtitles#burn-subtitles-into-the-picture).

## Hardware encoding fails

A hardware encoder needs a compatible device and runtime. macOS uses VideoToolbox options; supported Windows machines can use NVIDIA NVENC. Linux bundles use software encoders.

Try the corresponding software encoder to isolate the problem. Include the encoder name and the relevant log message when reporting a failure.

## Preview is unavailable or slow

Select a supported source and allow it to finish loading. High-resolution video, difficult codecs, and filters can increase preview work.

Try a shorter or smaller source and simplify the processing settings. Check a short conversion separately: the exported result and preview playback are different parts of the workflow.

## Report a reproducible problem

Open an issue on [Frame's GitHub repository](https://github.com/66HEX/frame/issues). Include your operating system, Frame version, source format and codecs, output settings, steps to reproduce, and the relevant log excerpt.

Remove private file paths and sensitive metadata from anything you share. If possible, provide a small sample that you have permission to publish.
